ers — consider a caption or description","bytes":16102},{"src":"https://cdc.govprojectOPHSS_CSELS_DPHID_MMWR_OD_EditorsMMWR-WECURRENT%20-%20May%2017Final%20to%20XMLbjx5@www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5914a3f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of 2009-10 laboratory-confirmed influenza-associated hospitalizations and cumulative hospitalization rates per 10,000 population, by age group and surveillance week from Emerging Infections Program (EIP), and new sites for the 2006-07, 2007-08, 2008-09, and 2009-10 influenza seasons. As of March 27, 2010, cumulative rates of laboratory-confirmed influenza-associated hospitalizations reported for children aged 0-4 years were 6.6 per 10,000 population by EIP and 10.5 per 10,000 population by the new sites.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":450,"renderedHeight":849,"naturalWidth":450,"naturalHeight":849,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 537 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":26825},{"src":"https://cdc.govprojectOPHSS_CSELS_DPHID_MMWR_OD_EditorsMMWR-WECURRENT%20-%20May%2017Final%20to%20XMLbjx5@www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5914a3f3.gif","alt":"The figure shows the percentage of all deaths attributed to pneumonia and influenza (P&I), by surveillance week and year for 122 U.S. cities from 2006-2010 from the Mortality Reporting System. For the week ending March 27, pneumonia or influenza was reported as an underlying or contributing cause of death for 7.9% of all deaths reported through the 122 Cities Mortality Reporting System, above the week-specific epidemic threshold of 7.8%.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":620,"renderedHeight":288,"naturalWidth":620,"naturalHeight":288,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 441 characters — cons